  "query_preview": "..Mostly sunny skies this afternoon for the majority of \nsoutheast Arizona as a band of cirrus clouds across eastern areas \nexits the region over the next few hours. This is associated with a \ndry trough passing through the region. Another chilly night is in \nstore tonight with morning low temperatures Tuesday around freezing \nin the normally colder valleys south and east of Tucson such as the \nSulphur Springs Valleys. \n\nA progressive weather pattern this week with shortwave ridging \nTuesday, followed by another trough Wednesday. This will bring with \nit more high level clouds but really no significant impact on our \nweather. As a surface high builds down the southern Rockies, the \ngradient will tighten enough for some easterly breezes in the \nnormally susceptible locations with gusts around 20 to 25 mph \nWednesday morning. \n\nThereafter, stronger ridging starts to build in on Thanksgiving Day \nand continues through the holiday weekend with continued dry \nconditions. Expect a return to above normal temperatures following \nthe period of near normal temperatures over the next few days. \n\nThe weather pattern looks to change around the middle of next week \nwith ensembles depicting some form of troughiness and associated \nvalley rain and mountain snow around next Wednesday/Thursday. Still \nlots of time to see how that evolves but with an expected active \nsubtropical jet this winter (aided by El Nino), this quiet weather \npattern is bound to break down sooner rather than later.",
